WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.900
Many .NET frameworks use reflection to bind data, construct objects, map properties, discover attributes, or call handlers.

2
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.800
That automation can reduce boilerplate and make applications easier to build.

3
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:21.180
It can also hide important security decisions if the team treats the framework behavior as automatic permission.

4
00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:32.340
Deserialization, model binding, command dispatch, object mapping, validation, and dependency injection should use explicit type boundaries and trusted contracts.

5
00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:37.160
A public request shape should be narrow enough to describe what the caller may provide.

6
00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:46.300
A domain model may contain fields, navigation properties, or state transitions that should not be bound directly from external input.

7
00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:53.620
Avoid broad domain models as automatic input targets when narrower DTOs or command models are safer.

8
00:00:53.720 --> 00:01:01.880
Do not let untrusted data choose arbitrary concrete types, handler classes, command names, formatter types, or service implementations.

9
00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:06.060
Runtime selection should come from a trusted registry or allowlist.

10
00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:13.700
Attribute-driven behavior should be reviewed because attributes can affect routing, validation, authorization, serialization, or exposure.

11
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:20.200
An attribute may look like metadata, but it can change how the framework treats a type or member.

12
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.500
Attribute use should be visible in code review and testing.

13
00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:31.780
Framework automation must still enforce input validation, authorization, tenant boundaries, data minimization, and safe error handling.

14
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:39.240
Reflection can make the plumbing flexible, but the security decisions must remain explicit enough to test and maintain.
